# AKS vs Self-Managed Kubernetes: Interview Guide

## 1. Three Ways to Create a Kubernetes Cluster

As a DevOps engineer working on Azure, there are three distinct approaches to running Kubernetes:

```
Option 1: On-Premises
  Private data center / OpenStack
  └── Virtual Machines (manual)
        ├── 3 VMs → Control Plane (master nodes)
        └── 2 VMs → Data Plane (worker nodes)

Option 2: Azure VMs (Self-Managed on Cloud)
  Azure Virtual Machine Service
  └── Virtual Machines (requested from Azure)
        ├── 3 VMs → Control Plane (manual setup)
        └── 2 VMs → Data Plane (manual setup)

Option 3: Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)
  Azure AKS Service
  └── Managed Control Plane (Azure handles this)
  └── Node Pool (VM Scale Sets) → Data Plane
```

**Options 1 and 2 are both self-managed Kubernetes clusters** — the DevOps team is responsible for all setup, upgrades, and maintenance.

**Option 3 (AKS) is an Azure-managed Kubernetes cluster** — Azure manages the control plane; the team only manages the node pools.

### Why 3 nodes for the control plane?

Three control plane nodes is the minimum recommended configuration for **high availability**. This ensures that if one master node fails, the cluster continues to operate.

---

## 2. Comparison Parameters

### 2.1 Maintenance

**Kubernetes version upgrades** happen regularly. Kubernetes typically releases 3 minor versions per year. Staying on an unsupported version introduces security vulnerabilities.

**Version notation:**

```
1   .   27   .   4
│       │        └── Z-stream (patch) — e.g., security fix
│       └────────── Y-stream (minor)  — new features
└────────────────── X-stream (major)  — breaking changes
```

| Option | Upgrade responsibility |
|---|---|
| **On-Premises** | Fully manual — team must upgrade Kubernetes AND the underlying OS on every node |
| **Azure VMs (self-managed)** | Kubernetes upgrades are manual; Azure may help with some OS-level patches but Kubernetes itself is the team's responsibility |
| **AKS** | Azure manages Z-stream (patch) upgrades automatically when configured; team controls Y-stream (minor) upgrades with scheduling |

> **Summary:** AKS offers significantly less maintenance overhead, especially for patch-level upgrades.

---

### 2.2 Cost

| Option | Cost profile |
|---|---|
| **On-Premises** | Low if existing infrastructure is being reused; very high if buying hardware, setting up a data center, and hiring system admins from scratch |
| **Azure VMs (self-managed)** | Moderate — pay for VMs, storage, and networking; costs depend heavily on right-sizing and autoscaling configuration |
| **AKS** | Control plane is cost-effective (managed by Azure); node pool (VM Scale Sets) cost depends on usage; autoscaling helps reduce waste |

> AKS charges only for the worker node VMs (node pool). The control plane is provided at no additional cost in standard AKS.

---

### 2.3 Scalability and Availability

| Option | Scaling approach |
|---|---|
| **On-Premises** | Manual or requires additional tooling; no built-in autoscaling out of the box |
| **Azure VMs (self-managed)** | Possible with manual autoscaling configuration; more complex to set up correctly |
| **AKS** | Built-in **cluster autoscaler** using VM Scale Sets; nodes scale in and out automatically based on pod demand |

**AKS node pool autoscaling configuration (from Day 15):**

- Min node count: `1`
- Max node count: `2` (or higher based on workload)
- Scale method: **Autoscale**

> Setting max node count too high can expose the cluster to cost spikes if a DDoS attack occurs — autoscaling keeps the application alive but Azure will bill for every node spun up.

---

### 2.4 Integrations

As Kubernetes has matured, it integrates with many external systems:

- **Ingress controllers** (e.g., NGINX, Azure Application Gateway)
- **CSI drivers** for persistent storage
- **Secret management** solutions (e.g., Azure Key Vault)
- **Microsoft Entra ID** (Azure AD) for RBAC
- **Load balancer services** (requires cloud controller manager)

| Option | Integration difficulty |
|---|---|
| **On-Premises** | High — load balancer type Services don't work out of the box (no cloud controller manager); projects like MetalLB exist but aren't fully production-ready for all scenarios |
| **Azure VMs (self-managed)** | Moderate — possible but requires manual configuration for each integration |
| **AKS** | Low — native integrations with Azure services (ACR, Key Vault, Entra ID, Application Gateway) are well-documented and straightforward to enable |

---

### 2.5 Security and Flexibility

This is where **on-premises and self-managed** have an advantage over AKS:

| Factor | On-Premises / Self-Managed | AKS |
|---|---|---|
| **Control over control plane** | Full — can customise API server flags, admission controllers, etcd configuration | Limited — control plane is managed by Azure |
| **Data residency** | Data stays entirely within own infrastructure | Data on public cloud (Azure handles compliance, but some organisations prefer on-prem for sensitive data) |
| **Firewall control** | Complete control over network perimeter | Managed within Azure VNet; less direct control |
| **Customisation** | Unlimited — install any CNI (Calico, Flannel, Cilium), custom admission webhooks, etc. | Some CNI choices and control plane customisations are restricted |

> Many organisations run application workloads on AKS but keep databases or sensitive data on-premises, bridging the two with VPN Gateway or VNet peering.

---

## 3. AKS — Automatic Upgrade Feature

When creating an AKS cluster, the **Automatic Upgrade** option can be configured:

| Setting | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| **Disabled** | No automatic upgrades — team manages all upgrades manually |
| **Patch** | AKS automatically applies Z-stream (patch) upgrades within the current minor version (e.g., 1.27.4 → 1.27.8) |
| **Stable** | AKS upgrades to the latest stable minor version |
| **Rapid** | AKS upgrades to the latest available version as soon as it is available |

**Upgrade schedule** can also be configured — for example, apply patches every Sunday night when traffic is lowest.

```
AKS Portal → Create Cluster → Cluster Details → Automatic upgrade
```

> Timely upgrades are critical. Running an unsupported version means known CVEs (vulnerabilities) are unpatched. AKS makes Z-stream upgrades largely hands-off.

---

## 4. Full Comparison Table

| Parameter | On-Premises | Azure VMs (Self-Managed) | AKS |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Control plane management** | Team | Team | Azure |
| **K8s version upgrades** | Fully manual | Fully manual | Automated (patch); scheduled (minor) |
| **OS/node patching** | Fully manual | Partially Azure-assisted | Azure-managed |
| **Cost** | Low (if infra exists); High (if building from scratch) | Moderate | Moderate; control plane free |
| **Autoscaling** | Manual setup required | Manual setup required | Built-in via VM Scale Sets |
| **Cloud integrations** | Limited / complex | Moderate | Native and straightforward |
| **Load balancer Services** | Not available out of the box | Available | Available |
| **Control plane customisation** | Full | Full | Limited |
| **Data residency control** | Full | Moderate | Depends on Azure region and compliance |
| **Recommended for beginners** | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ |
| **Recommended for startups** | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ |
| **Recommended for large enterprises (existing setup)** | ✅ (if on-prem exists) | ✅ | ✅ |

---

## 5. Which Option to Talk About in Interviews

### Talk about AKS if:

- I am a **beginner or intermediate** in Kubernetes.
- I work at a **startup** or an organization with a small DevOps team.
- My organization is **cloud-native** and the JD doesn't mention on-premises.
- I want to focus on learning Kubernetes concepts (deployments, services, Ingress, RBAC) without getting into cluster administration.

### Talk about self-managed Kubernetes (Azure VMs) if:

- I have **hands-on experience** with Kubernetes cluster upgrades, scaling, and configuration.
- I have worked with **Terraform or ARM templates** to provision clusters.
- I can speak to **cost optimisation** techniques for virtual machines.
- The job description mentions cluster administration, kubeadm, or self-hosted Kubernetes.

### Talk about on-premises Kubernetes only if:

- I have **genuine on-premises experience**.
- The **job description explicitly mentions** on-premises or hybrid cloud.
- My organization runs a **private cloud** (e.g., OpenStack, VMware).

> Most organisations today are on public cloud. Unless the JD specifies otherwise, it is safer to focus on AKS for Azure-focused roles.

---

## 6. Summary

```
Three Kubernetes approaches on Azure:

On-Premises          Azure VMs (Self-Managed)     AKS
──────────           ────────────────────────     ───
Full control         Full control                 Limited control plane control
High maintenance     High maintenance             Low maintenance
Cost: variable       Cost: moderate               Cost: moderate (free control plane)
No built-in scaling  Manual scaling               Built-in autoscaling
Complex integrations Moderate integrations        Native Azure integrations
Best for: orgs       Best for: experienced        Best for: beginners, startups,
with existing        teams, large enterprises     cloud-native teams
on-prem infra        with VM K8s history
```

**Key terms to remember:**

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Control plane | Master nodes — API server, etcd, scheduler, controller manager |
| Data plane | Worker nodes — where application pods actually run |
| Node pool | A group of VMs (VM Scale Sets) that form the worker nodes in AKS |
| Z-stream upgrade | Patch-level upgrade (e.g., 1.27.4 → 1.27.8) — bug fixes and CVEs |
| Y-stream upgrade | Minor version upgrade (e.g., 1.27 → 1.28) — new features |
| Cluster autoscaler | AKS feature that adds/removes nodes based on pod resource demand |

---
